// Tailnet access policy: each member's devices reach each other, the model
// server included, and nothing else gets in.
//
// Paste into https://login.tailscale.com/admin/acls/file. Pasting replaces the
// tailnet's whole policy.
//
// Defining `grants` makes the tailnet default-deny. This one grant lets every
// device a member owns reach that member's other devices, on any port. Another
// member's devices, and tagged devices, get nothing until a grant names them.
// The server joins as one of your devices (scripts/2-tailscale.sh), so it needs
// no grant of its own.

{
	"grants": [
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:member"],
			"dst": ["autogroup:self"],
			"ip":  ["*"],
		},
	],
}

// To run the server as a tagged device instead, owned by no one and reachable
// on named ports only: set LLM_TAG=tag:llm-server in config.env, apply the
// policy BEFORE scripts/2-tailscale.sh (a machine can only advertise a tag the
// policy names an owner for), and add to the policy above:
//
//	"tagOwners": {
//		"tag:llm-server": ["autogroup:admin"],
//	},
//
// and to "grants", with YOUR-LOGIN as shown on the admin console's Users page
// (for example alice@github or alice@example.com), and LLM_HTTPS_PORT in place
// of 443 if config.env changes it:
//
//	{
//		"src": ["YOUR-LOGIN"],
//		"dst": ["tag:llm-server"],
//		// Ports belong in `ip`. Appending them to `dst` is the older ACL
//		// syntax and does not work in a grant.
//		"ip": [
//			"tcp:443",  // the inference endpoint (tailscale serve)
//			"tcp:22",   // SSH, if Remote Login is on
//			"tcp:5900", // Screen Sharing, if on (README, step 6)
//		],
//	},
//
// Tagged devices never have their keys expire. A device you own does, unless
// you disable key expiry for it in the admin console (scripts/2-tailscale.sh
// reminds you).

// Also, in the admin console (not expressible in this file):
//   - DNS page: enable MagicDNS and HTTPS Certificates.
//     `tailscale serve` needs both.
//   - Settings -> Device management: enable Device approval, so a leaked auth
//     key alone is not enough to join the tailnet and reach the endpoint.
